  <h3>Euclidean distance</h3>

  <p>The straightforward one. Two vectors \(\mathbf{x}\) and \(\mathbf{y}\):</p>

  <p>\[d(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) = \lVert \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y} \rVert_2 = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{784} (x_i - y_i)^2}\]</p>

  <p>Small distance means the two images agree pixel by pixel. And that is exactly its weakness: it checks agreement <em>per coordinate</em>, not agreement in shape. Nudge the same stroke one pixel to the right and suddenly hundreds of coordinates disagree in pairs, every cell the ink left and every cell it moved into. You'd say “same digit” without hesitating. The distance balloons anyway. Here is that failure drawn out:</p>
