  <div class="callout">Once data lives in a space, meaning becomes something you can measure.</div>

  <hr class="sep">

  <h2>Why this matters for language</h2>

  <p>This is why the chapter started with images instead of words. Images show us, concretely, what a learning system needs from its data: a space where similar things sit near each other before any learning has happened.</p>

  <p>Now hold Unicode up against that standard. Stable identities, yes. But identities are isolated points. There is no distance between 65 and 66 that means anything, no angle, no neighbourhood. A learner staring at Unicode integers can ask exactly one question, same number or different number, and nothing interesting lives at that resolution.</p>

  <div class="callout">Learning requires a space where similarity is measurable.</div>

  <p>Images come with their space for free. Text doesn't. If language is going to be learnable the way images are, its space has to be <em>built</em>. That is precisely the job of embeddings, and it's where this series goes next.</p>

